The '''Kingdom of Burawa''' (Burawi: ''Boqortooyada Burawa'') was a sovereign state in [[Southwestern Kaftia]] that existed from 403 AD to 1832. Founded by the union of the [[Kingdom of Iskhal]] and the [[Republic of Mazar]], the kingdom grew from a coastal alliance to become the dominant power in the region, eventually establishing a colonial empire that spanned multiple continents. The kingdom was ruled by the [[House Kadim|Kadim dynasty]] throughout its 1,429-year history, making it one of the longest-lasting monarchies in Adonian history.

The kingdom's capital was initially located at [[Kaysoor]] on the Burawi coast, but was moved to [[Samara]] in 1452 during the reign of [[Axmed II]], facilitating closer control of the growing colonial empire. The official language was [[Burawi language|Burawi]], though regional languages including [[Dakari language|Dakari]] and [[Sarafoni language|Sarafoni]] were spoken in the northern territories.

The Kingdom of Burawa ended with the [[Rose Revolution]] of 1832, when [[Iacob VI]] abdicated following a peaceful revolution led by [[Siciid Dhegey]]. The monarchy was replaced by the [[First Republic of Burawa]], a parliamentary republic established by the [[Constitutional Convention of 1832]].

== History ==

{{main|History of Burawa}}

=== Formation (403 AD) ===

{{main|Mazar-Iskhal Alliance}}

The Kingdom of Burawa was formed in 403 AD through the union of two polities: the [[Kingdom of Iskhal]], a monarchy controlling the Buran River valley, and the [[Republic of Mazar]], a merchant republic on the Mesogean coast. The alliance was sealed by the marriage of [[Bugad of Iskhal|Bugad]], heir to the Iskhal throne, to [[Appa of Mazar]], daughter of the leading family of the Mazar Clique.

The formation of the kingdom represented the culmination of centuries of interaction between the highland interior and the coastal trading cities. Iskhal provided military strength and agricultural resources; Mazar provided maritime expertise and commercial networks. The united kingdom was positioned to dominate the southern Kaftian coast and to project power inland toward the highlands.

=== Medieval period (403–1452) ===

{{main|Medieval History of Burawa}}

The medieval period saw the consolidation of Burawi power and the expansion of the kingdom's territory. The early kings of the [[Urtog dynasty]] (the royal house established by Bugad) focused on securing the Buran River valley and establishing control over the approaches to the Mesogean coast. The kingdom's strategic position, controlling access to the interior highlands, made it a significant player in regional politics.

The medieval period was marked by conflict with neighboring states, particularly the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] in the highlands. The Ufyen kingdom, which had ruled the highlands since ancient times, viewed Burawi expansion as a threat. A series of conflicts, collectively known as the Orma-Burawa wars, established Burawi dominance in the coastal regions while leaving the highlands under Ufyen control.

=== Imperial period (1452–1620) ===

{{main|Imperial History of Burawa}}

The imperial period began with the relocation of the capital from Kaysoor to Samara in 1452. The move reflected the kingdom's changing strategic orientation: from a coastal kingdom focused on Mesogean trade to an imperial power with colonial possessions across the Kaftian world. The discovery of [[Lurandia]] and the founding of [[Lacashe]] in 1516 marked the beginning of Burawa's colonial empire.

The [[Colonial Expansion of Burawa]] (1516–1620) saw the establishment of colonial possessions in [[Abyala]] and North Kaftia. The kingdom's navy, rebuilt after the [[War of the Mesogean]] (1590–1610), protected colonial trade routes and projected Burawi power across the seas. The colonial empire brought enormous wealth to the kingdom, financing a cultural flowering and the expansion of state institutions.

=== Late Colonial period (1620–1709) ===

{{main|Late Colonial History of Burawa}}

The late colonial period was marked by the consolidation of colonial governance and the emergence of intellectual currents that would later be recognised as precursors to the [[Enlightenment|era of enlightenment]]. The period was dominated by the [[Kadim-Galwa]] cadet branch, which provided three kings: [[Nuur III]] (r. 1608–1655), [[Faarax IV]] (r. 1655–1690), and [[Andrias III]] (r. 1690–1709).

The period saw the [[Treaty of Ibadan]] with Breisland, the [[Aethiopean War]] with Volisania, and the [[Abyalan Anti-Piracy Campaign]]. The death of Andrias III without an heir in 1709 precipitated the [[Burawa Imperial Split]], dividing the kingdom between the [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] and [[Kadim-Galwa]] branches.

=== Four House Era (1709–1743) ===

{{main|Four House Era}}

The Four House Era followed the Imperial Split, with [[Iacob V]] ruling the eastern Kingdom of Burawa. The king had been compelled to create a four-house parliament to secure support for his accession, establishing a representative body that shared power with the crown. The period was marked by parliamentary gridlock, the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1816), and the [[Restoration Revolution]] (1741–1742), which resulted in Iacob V's assassination.

The [[Constitution of 1743]] established a limited monarchy under [[Nuur V]], inaugurating the Constitutional Era.

=== Constitutional Era (1743–1800) ===

{{main|Constitutional Era of Burawa}}

The Constitutional Era, also known as the Great Nuur Period, was marked by the operation of a limited monarchy under parliamentary supremacy. [[Nuur V]] reigned for nearly six decades, providing stability that allowed for scientific advancement, cultural expression, and economic growth. The period saw the extension of citizenship to non-Catholics, the development of railroads and steamships, and the growth of Burawi industrial capacity.

The era ended with Nuur V's death on 16 March 1800. The kingdom had grown prosperous and stable, but the constitutional order would prove unable to survive the crises of the following decades.

=== Burbur Era and end of the monarchy (1800–1832) ===

{{main|Burbur Era}}

The Burbur Era, named for the Burawi word meaning "collapse" or "breakdown," encompassed the reign of [[Iacob VI]], the last Burawi monarch. The king's autocratic tendencies brought him into conflict with parliament, while Hoga factionalism paralyzed the legislative process. The [[Burawi War]] (1811–1822) and the [[Maianka War]] (1828–1831) drained the kingdom's resources and exposed the fragility of the political order.

The exposure of the [[Hoga Plot]], a conspiracy between the king and traditionalist aristocrats to restore absolutism, discredited both the monarchy and the traditionalist faction. In 1832, [[Siciid Dhegey]] led the [[Rose Revolution]], a peaceful revolution that forced Iacob VI's abdication in the king's rose garden. The monarchy that had ruled Burawa since 403 AD was at an end. The [[First Republic of Burawa]] was proclaimed later that year.

== Geography ==

{{main|Geography of Burawa}}

The Kingdom of Burawa occupied the southwestern portion of the Kaftian peninsula. The kingdom's territory extended from the Mesogean coast in the south to the Great Kaftian Lake in the north, and from the Aethiopean Ocean coast in the west to the Orma highlands in the east. The Great Kaftian Mountain Range divided the kingdom into northern and southern zones with distinct climates and economies.

The southern portion of the kingdom, nearest the Mesogean Sea, had a drier, temperate climate with fertile river valleys. The major rivers, including the Buran, Dafar, Hooyal, and Galbeed, provided water for agriculture and transportation. The capital at Samara was located in the Buran River valley, approximately 50 kilometers from the coast.

The northern portion of the kingdom, above the mountain range, was hotter and wetter, characterized by savanna and, in the far northeast, rainforest. The Great Kaftian Lake, one of the largest lakes on Adonia, dominated the northern landscape and provided a transportation route to the Aethiopean Ocean.

== Government ==

{{main|Government of Burawa}}

=== Monarchy ===

The Kingdom of Burawa was an absolute monarchy for most of its history, with power concentrated in the hands of the king. The [[Kadim dynasty]] ruled the kingdom from its founding in 403 until its end in 1832, making it one of the longest-reigning dynasties in Adonian history. The dynasty was divided into several cadet branches, including Kadim-Kaysoor, Kadim-Mazar, and Kadim-Galwa, which competed for the throne at various points.

The king served as head of state, commander-in-chief of the military, and highest judicial authority. Royal decrees had the force of law, though in practice the king relied upon the cooperation of the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy, the Church, and, increasingly, the merchant class to govern effectively.

=== Parliament ===

{{main|Parliament of Burawa}}

Parliamentary government was introduced in stages. The four-house parliament, created in 1709 under Iacob V, comprised the House of the King, the House of the Church, the House of the Hogas, and the House of the Landowners. This arrangement proved unwieldy, and the Constitution of 1743 reduced the parliament to two houses: the Hoga Senate and the House of Commons.

The franchise was limited to men who owned land or paid the poll tax. The Hoga Senate represented the hereditary aristocracy; the House of Commons represented the broader propertied classes, including merchants and professionals.

=== Administrative divisions ===

{{main|Administrative divisions of Burawa}}

The kingdom was divided into regions, each governed by appointed officials or, in some cases, by Hoga lords who exercised hereditary authority over their territories. The major regions included Burawa proper (the Buran River valley), Dakare, Sarafone, Ayorin, Kushish, Maianka, Sabia, Iracwi, Sago, and Dimar.

Colonial territories were administered separately from the metropolitan regions. [[Lacashe]], [[Bindorama]], and the North Kaftian forts were governed by royal appointees with broad discretionary authority.

== Economy ==

The Burawi economy evolved significantly over the kingdom's 1,429-year history. In the medieval period, the economy was primarily agricultural, with the Buran River valley providing grain, and the highland trade providing minerals and pastoral products. The coastal cities, particularly Mazar, engaged in maritime trade with the Mesogean world.

The colonial period transformed the Burawi economy. Colonial possessions in Abyala and North Kaftia provided plantation products, raw materials, and, until 1789, enslaved persons. The triangle trade, connecting Kaftia, Abyala, and the Mesogean markets, generated enormous wealth for the kingdom. The merchant class grew in wealth and political influence, eventually rivaling the traditional Hoga aristocracy.

Industrialisation began in the late Constitutional Era and accelerated during the early republic. Textile manufacturing, railroads, steamships, and telegraph lines transformed Burawi society and economy in the decades before the monarchy's fall.

== Religion ==

{{main|Burawi Catholic Church}}

The official religion of the Kingdom of Burawa was the [[Burawi Catholic Church]], a distinct communion within the broader Catholic tradition. The Church was established as part of the Mesogean cultural influence that arrived via contact with Aetolian and later Volisanian traders and missionaries. The Church exercised significant influence over Burawi society, operating schools, registering vital records, and providing moral guidance to the population.

The Cardinal of Burawa, seated at [[Weli Markos]] following the Rose Revolution, served as head of the Church and as a significant political figure. The Church's role in politics evolved over time: from a pillar of royal legitimacy to a mediator between factions to a civil institution in the republican period.

Non-Catholic religions were tolerated to varying degrees throughout the kingdom's history. The extension of citizenship to non-Catholics during the Constitutional Era allowed Jewish and Protestant merchants to participate fully in Burawi economic and political life.

== Legacy ==

The Kingdom of Burawa left a profound legacy on the Burawi people and on southwestern Kaftia more broadly. The kingdom's 1,429-year existence created a sense of Burawi national identity that transcended the political divisions of later periods. The Burawi language, standardised during the medieval period, remains the primary language of the region. The borders established by the kingdom largely define the modern Burawi state.

The Kadim dynasty, though no longer ruling, remains a symbol of Burawi continuity. The dynasty's cadet branches continue to exist, and some members of the family remain prominent in Burawi public life.

The kingdom's colonial empire, though largely dissolved, left lasting impacts on the former colonial territories. Lacashe, independent since 1789, maintains cultural and economic ties with Burawa. The North Kaftian territory of [[Nugal]] remained under Burawi control into the republican period.

